thank you thank you very much Mina and  every morning - to everyone as Mina has  already said the Commission has taken a  number of infringement decisions today  and as was mentioned also quite some of  them come under my portfolio because  there are environmental infringement  amongst the tasks which President  Juncker gave me for my mandate was to  improve the implementation of our  environmental legislation infringement  procedures are a very essential part of  this in the cases where Member States  despite the dialogue that we have with  them and the support that we give them  do not implement and do not enforce our  commonly agreed rules this commission  does not take decisions lightly we weigh  the options carefully and we are never  happy to go to court but sometimes it's  the only way it is what it takes to  achieve results during the course of my  mandate we'll have been very vigilant on  nature protection and today we are  asking many member states to step up  their efforts in this regard the Natura  2000 network of protected areas is on  the frontline in our fight to protect  biodiversity but there are still  important gaps in the network itself and  in the way that the protected areas are  actually managed and this is why we have  decided to today to refer Greece to the  court but also to click in steps against  a number of other member states and we  also take legal steps against some  Member States for not properly  protecting a series of specific  protected species and their habitats we  are also fighting to protect tulips  waters that is why we have referred  Sweden to the court today Albin  wastewater must be of a standard that  poses no threat upon reintroducing it  into the environment and we are sending  a warning to Germany that further  measures are necessary to implement  ruling on protecting our waters from  nitrate pollution but one thing in  particular stands out throughout my  mandate I have always been very clear  about the importance of human health in  this area there is no room for  compromise and it's especially true of  air pollution  last year in 2018 we set out a new  approach to the problem stepping up  engagements with member states to  protect citizens health and the  communication which we called a Europe  that protects cleaner for all one year  later the evidence of harm from air  pollution still continues to grow there  is no more room for excuses we need to  see decisive action today for example we  are calling Bulgaria and Spain before  the EU Court of Justice for problems for  issues with air pollution in the case of  Bulgaria the referral is for so2 for  sulphur dioxide the daily and the hourly  limits are persistently being exceeded  and the southeast zone limits that  should have been observed since  accession way back in 2007 for Spain the  reference is for a persistent breach of  EU rules on nitrogen dioxide and no.2  which have been legally binding since  2010 the problem areas are basically  three mainly three Madrid Barcelona and  balanced by ich leo Braga  we are also accelerating infringement  procedures for other member states for  the air quality monitoring network in  Romania for particulate matter in Poland  and for the state of air legislation in  Croatia we are taking these steps  because air pollution is still the  biggest environmental health problem in  the European Union responsible for some  400  thousand premature deaths each and every  year it also causes a range of health  problems including asthma cardiovascular  diseases as well as lung cancer and it's  a major economic burden as well with  direct cost of over twenty billion euros  each year these problems need to be  solved and they can be solved when the  political will is dead the Commission is  helping whenever it can facilitating  dialogue and funding programs that  tackle air pollution but there are still  places that need to take stronger action  as soon as possible  infringement cases and an appearance in  court can bring that point home EU  legislation is not bureaucratic it's not  abstract and it's not about what happens  here in Brussels  it's about solving real problems it's  about improving people's lives wherever  they are and that's why we are here  today thank you very much thank you very  much commissioner
 

